.Dd November 14, 2026
.Dt RTC 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm rtc
.Nd the CMOS real-time clock
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/rtc0
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The device is the machine's CMOS clock, which keeps the date and time while
the machine is off and which the kernel reads once at boot to set the time;
under KVM the boot takes the time from the wall clock KVM states instead,
as Linux does, and reads the device only when asked.
The kernel writes it only while an NTP daemon keeps the time synchronized,
as
.Xr adjtimex 2
describes.
It answers Linux's ioctls for reading and setting the clock and its alarm,
with a
.Vt struct rtc_time
of nine ints: the second, minute, hour, day of the month, month from 0,
years since 1900, day of the week from Sunday, day of the year from 0, and
a daylight flag, which is 0.
.Bl -tag -width RTC_WKALM_SET
.It Dv RTC_RD_TIME
Read the clock, as the boot does: two samples that agree.
.It Dv RTC_SET_TIME
Set the clock, in the encoding and hour format its status register gives,
with its updates held while the registers are written, and the day of the
week with them.
The time the kernel keeps is not changed; see
.Xr clock_settime 2 .
.It Dv RTC_ALM_SET
Set the alarm to a time of day, the second, minute and hour of the struct,
on today's date, or tomorrow's when the time is before now, and disable it,
as Linux does.
.It Dv RTC_ALM_READ
Read the alarm's date and time.
.It Dv RTC_AIE_ON , RTC_AIE_OFF
Enable and disable the alarm.
An alarm whose time is not after now comes at once when it is enabled, as
on Linux.
.It Dv RTC_WKALM_SET , RTC_WKALM_RD
Set and read the wake alarm, a
.Vt struct rtc_wkalrm :
a byte that enables it, a pending byte, which a read gives as 0, as
Linux's does, and its date and time.
A set with the first byte 0 sets the time and disables the alarm.
.El
.Pp
The alarm is a date and time, which the clock raises its interrupt, IRQ 8,
at while the alarm is enabled.
The CMOS clock has no day alarm, so an enabled alarm must lie within a day
ahead, as Linux's driver requires.
An alarm comes once: then it is disabled, its interrupt off, and
.Dv RTC_WKALM_RD
and
.Dv RTC_ALM_READ
still give its date and time, which only a set changes.
Before the first set, the alarm is disabled at the clock's own alarm's time
of day, on the next day that time falls on.
.Pp
A
.Xr read 2
of exactly four bytes, or of an unsigned long's or more, eight bytes on
x86-64, waits for the alarm and gives that many: the
alarms since the last read in the bits above the low eight, which enabling
or setting the alarm does not clear, with
.Dv RTC_AF ,
0x20, and
.Dv RTC_IRQF ,
0x80;
.Xr poll 2
reports it readable once an alarm has come.
An alarm also ends a suspend to idle, which
.Xr rtcwake 1
starts through
.Pa /sys/power/state ;
see
.Xr sysfs 5 .
.Pp
The clock keeps UTC, as the kernel reads it, and two digits of the year, for
the years 2000 to 2099.
The device is root's to open, mode 0600, as Linux's is.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EACCES
A setting ioctl by a caller without privilege, as Linux answers it.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Dv RTC_SET_TIME
with a time out of range or a year outside 2000 to 2099;
.Dv RTC_WKALM_SET
or
.Dv RTC_ALM_SET
with a date or time that is not one, as Linux's
.Fn rtc_valid_tm
judges: a year before 1970 or past 2147483647, a month outside 0 to 11, a
day outside the month, an hour past 23, or a minute or second past 59,
which leaves the alarm as it was; an alarm enabled for more than a day less
a second ahead, which leaves it disabled; a read of another count below an
unsigned long's; or a
.Xr write 2
of the device.
The clock's update and periodic interrupts are not given.
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
A non-blocking read with no alarm come.
.It Bq Er EIO
.Dv RTC_RD_TIME ,
or the first use of the alarm, of a clock that holds no time, such as one
whose battery went; or an alarm enabled or
.Dv RTC_ALM_SET
while the clock holds none.
.It Bq Er ENOTTY
Another ioctl.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr busybox-util-linux 1 ,
.Xr adjtimex 2 ,
.Xr clock_settime 2 ,
.Xr sysfs 5
